{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"rg -n 'def test_.*(restored|later_writer|routed)|def _routed_unmoved|def _settled_row|routed.*restored|restored.*routed' tests\\\\test_intake_settled.py project-trajectory\\\\scripts\\\\acceptance_record.py; Get-Content -Raw CLAUDE.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -Raw docs\\\\reviews\\\\wi-963\\\\013-ADJUDICATE-93526df.md; Get-Content -Raw C:\\\\Projects\\\\ai-template.wt\\\\review-tmp\\\\2026-11-03a-leg31\\\\builder2\\\\spine-change-list.md\"","aggregated_output":"# WI-963 dispute sitting at 93526df\n\nDISPUTE: findings=F1;F2\n\n**Disclosure.** I held checkpoint sittings 008 and 009, which approved LLR-364 and TC-403 with the arm both findings name. Sitting 008 also accepted the rationale sentence F2 calls false. I judge that sentence below as an error I passed.\n\n## The behaviour both findings describe\n\n`acceptance_record._settled_row` settles a re-attested row in one of two ways. If no range-added entry after its latest re-attestation wrote its registry (`_no_later_writer`), it settles. If a later entry did, it settles only when the row's routed traced cells at the range's after revision equal those at its before revision (`_routed_unmoved`).\n\nThe findings' sequence:\n1. A lane re-points SR-001's `SN-Refs`, and act A re-attests the row.\n2. The lane restores `SN-Refs` to its pre-range value.\n3. Act B, re-attesting another row of the same registry, copies the registry.\n\nB's copy holds the restored value, so the row equals its copy. Its routed cells equal those at the range's start, so it settles and no amendment row mints. I read this from the code and the cited cases, and the reviewers' probes agree. The behaviour is real.\n\n## What the behaviour actually leaves unreported\n\n- **The routed restore was re-anchored by the kit's existing rule.** The restore is a traced change. The snapshot refresh lets any act re-anchor traced pointer changes without a verdict (its own refusal text: \"Traced cells ... and the ref pointers are never blocked here\"). That rule is the kit's standing traced-refresh policy, not this lane's. Only approved-cell drift is refused unread, so B could not have absorbed an approved change.\n- **Across the merged range nothing routed moved.** LLR-153's routed trigger is defined over the range's diff, so it has nothing to report even before this lane. The approved text change is exactly what A re-attested.\n- **What is left is narrow.** A's sitting read the row's chain with the new parent pointer, and the row lands with the old one. That is a context the blessing did not see, but it is a pointer the snapshot already re-anchors without review. Before this lane, the same range minted only a redundant approved-text row, which the old workaround closed by citing the act.\n\n## What each remedy costs\n\nEvery hand landing is a single squash commit, and the replay range Done-when item 6 names (`5245ac82c..08bbb71b6`) is one commit. On such a range, the re-attesting act's own copy is overwritten by the later copy and cannot be recovered.\n\n- **F1's remedy:** evidence bound to the act's own copy. On a squash it cannot be met without an IF-220 format change, one recording each re-attested row's state, which this lane's scope and its adopters' ledgers do not carry. Without that change it reduces to minting.\n- **F2's remedy:** delete the arm. That makes every later writer of the registry unsettle the row.\n\nEither way, the standard act turn mints again. An amendment act followed by a first-approval act in the same registry is the two-part act every adjudication lane takes. WI-978's act would have re-minted TC-158, for example. So would the WI-926 replay that Done-when item 6 requires to mint nothing.\n\nThat is the redundancy this work item exists to remove. It would come back on the common path to catch a rare sequence (restore a routed pointer between two acts in one registry) whose content the kit's traced-refresh rule already admits.\n\n## Where the lane is genuinely wrong\n\nThe text claims more than the arm delivers, and the Done-when is now ambiguous.\n- **The detail sentence.** LLR-364's detail says the second arm proves \"that nothing routed moved for a later copy to absorb\". It proves only that the routed cells are the same at the range's two ends. That is a false statement in an approved cell (K1), and I passed it at sitting 008.\n- **Done-when item 5.** It promises that a row \"edited after its act and restored to its pre-range text in one cell, still mints (the whole-row comparison)\". It holds when no later act copies the row's registry. It does not say it is bounded there, so read literally it collides with item 6.\n\nThe fix is to state the bound honestly, not to change the behaviour:\n- amend LLR-364's detail and rationale, and TC-403, through their authority, to say what the arm guarantees and its one limit: a routed pointer edited and restored between two acts copying the registry is re-anchored by the later copy, as any traced refresh is;\n- add the restore-under-a-later-writer case to TC-403, pinned as settling, so the bound is driven rather than assumed;\n- amend Done-when item 5 through its in-lane blessing so it is scoped to a range with no later copy of the row's registry;\n- correct the RESYNC entry to match.\n\nAn owner who prefers redundant mints to this bound can overrule the coordinator's D-014 on review. The verdict leaves that choice where D-014's record puts it.\n\n## F1\n\nF1 asks for settlement evidence bound to the row's own re-attesting act. On the squashed landings every hand landing is, that evidence does not exist without a ledger format change, so the remedy collapses into minting on every later writer. That restores the redundant mint this lane removes, on the standard two-part act and on the replay Done-when item 6 requires, to report a net-zero traced change the snapshot already re-anchors. The text defect it touches is ruled under F2.\n\n## F2\n\nThe behaviour stays. F2's K1 point holds: LLR-364 asserts a guarantee (\"nothing routed moved\") that the arm does not provide, and Done-when item 5 reads as a promise the arm breaks under a later writer. The deletion it proposes is the wrong fix, for the cost above.\n\nRULING: F1 DISMISS not-worth-cost evidence bound to the re-attesting act's own copy is unrecoverable on squashed landings without an IF-220 format change, so the remedy degrades to minting every row a later act's registry copy touches, re-minting the standard amendment-then-first-approval act turn and the WI-926 replay Done-when item 6 requires, to report a net-zero routed restore that the snapshot's traced-refresh rule already lets any act re-anchor\nRULING: F2 FIX LLR-364's detail claims the later-writer arm proves nothing routed moved, but it proves only equality at the range's two ends, and Done-when item 5 reads as unbounded; keep the arm, amend LLR-364, TC-403 and the RESYNC entry to state its guarantee and its one limit (a routed pointer restored between two acts copying the registry is re-anchored by the later copy), pin that case in TC-403, and scope Done-when item 5 through its in-lane blessing to a range with no later copy of the row's registry\n\r\n# WI-963 spine change list (fix round 2): proposed wording only, not applied\n\nNeither cell below was edited in the lane; both go through their authority (in-lane adjudication).\n\n## LLR-364 `detail`: proposed replacement of its second sentence\n\nBefore (the sentence ruled false):\n\n> A later act copying the registry without re-attesting the row cannot have absorbed an unattested change to its approved cells, only to its routed traced cells, so the first arm proves the copy is the re-attesting act's own and the second that nothing routed moved for a later copy to absorb, whether the range is one squashed commit or many.\n\nProposed:\n\n> A later act copying the registry without re-attesting the row cannot have absorbed an unattested change to its approved cells, only to its routed traced cells, so the first arm proves the copy is the re-attesting act's own and the second proves the row's routed traced cells are the same at the range's two ends, whether the range is one squashed commit or many. The second arm's one limit: a routed traced cell moved before the re-attesting act and restored after it is re-anchored by a later act's copy of the registry, as any traced refresh is, and the row settles.\n\nThe first sentence's clause \"or the row's routed traced cells at the after revision equal those at the before revision\" already states the arm as end equality and can stay.\n\n## LLR-364 `rationale`: proposed appended sentence\n\n> The later-writer arm accepts one bound rather than minting on every later copy of the registry: a routed traced cell restored between two acts is re-anchored by the later copy, as the snapshot already re-anchors any traced change without a verdict.\n\n## TC-403 `method`: proposed replacement of its later-writer clause\n\nBefore:\n\n> ...; a later act copying the same registry, or a later legacy act, leaves the row settled only when its routed traced cells are unchanged across the range; and the replayed landings mint nothing.\n\nProposed:\n\n> ...; a later act copying the same registry, or a later legacy act, leaves the row settled only when its routed traced cells are the same at the range's two ends; the arm's one limit is pinned: a routed traced cell moved before the re-attesting act and restored after it settles when a later act re-attesting another row copies the same registry, over an unsquashed and a squashed range, while the same restore with no later copy of the registry still mints over both; and the replayed landings mint nothing.\n\n## TC-403 `expected`: proposed\n\n> Satisfies SR-178 AcceptanceCriteria through the LLR-364 filtering contract, both arms of its later-writer condition and the second arm's stated limit included over squashed and unsquashed ranges, and IF-091 call outcome.\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -Raw docs\\\\work\\\\active\\\\wi-963\\\\WI-963-sweep-skips-settled-amendments.md; Get-Content -Raw docs\\\\requirements\\\\open-items.toml\"","aggregated_output":"+++\nid = \"WI-963\"\ntitle = \"The trunk mint never mints an amendment row for a row the merged range already settled\"\nworkstream = \"process\"\nsr_refs = [\"SR-178\"]\nspecref = \"docs/plans/2026-10-03-s11-in-lane-adjudication.md#42-the-re-mint-trap\"\nneeds = [\"OI-115\", \"WI-967\", \"OI-119\"]\nbuildtier = \"medium\"\nsafety_class = \"ordinary\"\nplanmode = \"single\"\npriority = 5\n+++\n\n## Context\n\nFiled by hand by the attended coordinator on 2026-10-20, split from WI-810 by\nthe owner's ruling (coordinator-2026-10-20 D-009); WI-810's remainder is\ncancelled. It is the first build after headless leg 06.\n\n**The defect.** When a lane lands, the trunk mint (`intake._routed_amendments`,\ncalled by `intake_after_merge` from the merge slot's post-merge arm and by\n`intake.py sweep` after a coordinator's hand landing) diffs the approved and\nrouted cells of the spine registries across the merge and mints an\n`adjudicate:` row for every row whose text moved. It never asks whether the\nlane already judged that change. Since in-lane adjudication (WI-849, WI-912),\na lane amends a row, sits it, and re-attests it in its own act; the row's\nStatus does not change, so the mint sees changed text and files a redundant\nrow. The coordinator then closes it \"as already settled\", citing the act.\nThat has happened 54 times (WI-785 on 2026-10-03 to WI-959 on 2026-10-20;\n`git log --grep=\"already settled\"`), each burning a WI id and a close commit,\nand the coordinator-cycle skill carries the workaround as a standing landing\nstep (\"close the re-mint citing the act\", SKILL section 4 step 3; recipes\nsection 3).\n\n**The design** is the S11 plan's section 4.2, never built: drop an amendment\nrecord when an act-ledger entry the merged range added re-attests the row\n(`acceptance_record.reattested_between` already reads this) AND the row's\nchanged cells at the merged tree equal their copies in the approved snapshot\n(`docs/archive/last_approved/`) at that same tree. The second test keeps the\nmint for a row amended again after its act. The comparison is the row's whole\napproved (and routed traced) cell set against its snapshot copy, not only the\ncells the range's net diff reports: a cell edited after the act and then\nrestored to its pre-range value is absent from the net diff yet differs from\nthe snapshot, and must still mint (coordinator-2026-10-20 D-013).\n\nAnchor: SR-178 (\"text that has moved away from its acceptance record is\nreported\"): a row re-attested in the range has not moved away from its\nrecord, so routing it again reports nothing. LLR-153 (the unified trunk-side\nintake mint) stays the code-home LLR.\nWI ids stay trunk-minted (revision 33); this row changes which rows the trunk\nmint files, not where ids come from.\n\n## Deliverables and order\n\nFour pieces, landing in this order within the one lane:\n\n1. **The filter** (one shared filter for the merge slot and `intake.py\n   sweep`), its regressions, and its LLR/TC rows passed through in-lane\n   adjudication. It lands first: everything below describes or depends on it.\n2. **The shared-walk clarification** (the comment above `AMENDMENT_CSVS` and\n   LLR-158's detail). It could land alone as text, but it states that the mint\n   drops re-attested rows, which is false until the filter lands, so it rides\n   with the filter rather than preceding it.\n3. **The workaround's removal** from the coordinator-cycle skill and its\n   recipes. It lands after the filter and its regressions pass: removing the\n   \"close the re-mint citing the act\" step before the mint stops filing those\n   rows would leave every landing with an unclosed redundant row.\n4. **The RESYNC_PACK entry**, last, anchored at the trunk commit that carries\n   the filter, since it describes the landed behaviour to adopters.\n\nThey stay one row because pieces 2 to 4 are each true only once piece 1 has\nlanded, and splitting them would file three rows whose only content is\nfollowing piece 1 (the coordinator's answer to S1 at critique round 1).\n\n## Done-when\n\n- An approved row amended in the merged range, re-attested by an act-ledger\n  entry the range added, and whose changed cells at the merged tree equal\n  their approved-snapshot copies there, yields no amendment row, at the merge\n  slot's post-merge arm and at `intake.py sweep` alike (one shared filter).\n- A row re-attested in the range and then amended again after its act still\n  yields its amendment row.\n- An approved row amended with no act in the range still yields its\n  amendment row, exactly as today.\n- An ABSENT act ledger reads as no acts, as `_ledger_acts` already reads it\n  (a fresh adopter has none before its first signing), so nothing is\n  filtered and the mint runs exactly as today; the filter is consulted only\n  when the range carries amendment records. A ledger or snapshot copy that is\n  present but cannot be read or parsed refuses the mint by name, as\n  `reattested_between`'s refusal already does. A row the ledger re-attests\n  whose snapshot copy is missing is not proven settled, so it is minted,\n  never skipped.\n- A row re-attested in the range, then edited after its act and restored to\n  its pre-range text in one cell, still mints (the whole-row comparison).\n- A regression replays two real landings that minted a redundant row (WI-926's\n  landing `08bbb71b6`, which minted WI-952, and WI-956's, which minted\n  WI-959) and mints none of their rows, while a fixture amended after its act\n  still mints.\n- LLR-153's detail (or a new LLR under SR-178, if the in-lane adjudicator\n  rules the rule a separate obligation) states the filter, a TC verifies each\n  arm, and the set passes in-lane adjudication.\n- The shared-walk invariant is restated where it lives: the comment above\n  `AMENDMENT_CSVS` (`acceptance_record.py`) and LLR-158's detail say the\n  pre-commit warn (`staged_spine_findings`) and the mint read one walk, and\n  that the mint additionally drops rows the merged range re-attested; the\n  warn itself is unchanged (it fires before any act).\n- The coordinator-cycle skill and its recipes drop \"close the re-mint citing\n  the act\" from the landing steps, in the same lane, after the filter and its\n  regressions pass (Deliverables and order).\n- RESYNC_PACK: one entry anchored at a trunk commit; an adopter's trunk mint\n  stops filing rows for amendments its lanes already settled. No data\n  migrates.\n- Coverage bound: the promise reaches the ledgers, snapshot copies and ranges\n  that supported workflows and the kit's own routes produce (the act turn, an\n  owner's signing act, a lane landing through the merge slot, `intake.py\n  sweep` over a landed range), as `project-trajectory/PROCESS.md` §6 \"Review\n  threat model\" bounds a spec's promise. A hand-edited act ledger or snapshot\n  copy, hand-chosen name aliases (a row id or registry path in another case\n  or spelling) and sub-second timing windows are outside it.\n- Review bar: A+B (REVIEW-A plus an independent REVIEW-B).\n\n## Trust\n\nThe act ledger and the approved snapshot gain one new authority: suppressing\nan adjudication row the mint would otherwise file. The guard follows\n`project-trajectory/PROCESS.md` §3, \"When a guard is owed\" (both carriers are\nfiles on disk, read at a git revision).\n\n- **Producer:** the act ledger (`docs/archive/last_approved/acts.toml`) and the\n  approved snapshot are written by\n  `project-trajectory/scripts/baseline_snapshot.py` `copy_live` (IF-123; the\n  ledger entry's shape is IF-220), reached through\n  `project-trajectory/scripts/intake.py snapshot` by the act turn\n  (`project-trajectory/scripts/coordinator_adjudicate.py act`) and by the\n  owner's own signing acts, and land with the lane or the owner's commit.\n  Every consumer below reads them at a git revision, never from the working\n  tree. Failed-write shapes:\n  - *Structured error:* `copy_live` refuses (drift outside the act's write\n    set, an unknown id, a malformed existing ledger) before any copy and\n    writes nothing; there is nothing to commit.\n  - *Failed call or timeout* (a crashed or killed act turn): the registry\n    copies are written before the ledger entry is appended, so an\n    interrupted act leaves either no change or copies with no new ledger\n    entry. Nothing reaches a consumer unless that working tree is committed;\n    if it is, the range adds no act for those rows and they mint.\n  - *Partial file:* a truncated ledger does not parse, and the mint refuses\n    by name; a truncated or partial snapshot copy either does not parse (the\n    mint refuses by name), or lacks the row or no longer equals its cells\n    (the row mints).\n- **Consumers newly given the authority, by path:**\n  - `project-trajectory/scripts/intake.py`: the filter's one home in\n    `_routed_amendments` (or a helper it alone calls), through\n    `_amendment_drafts` and `intake_after_merge`, reached by\n    `project-trajectory/scripts/integrate.py` (`integrate_one`'s post-merge\n    arm, in the held merge slot) and by `intake.py sweep` (`_cmd_sweep`, a\n    range sweep after a hand landing).\n  - `project-trajectory/scripts/acceptance_record.py`, the intermediary the\n    filter reads through: `_ledger_acts` / `_added_acts` /\n    `reattested_between` for the ledger at each revision, and the row read of\n    a snapshot copy at the after revision. Two existing behaviours there\n    collapse a failure into absence: `_ledger_acts` reads any failed `git\n    show` (`kitlib` `git_out` returns None on any failure) as an absent\n    ledger, and `_spine_rows_at` reads an unparseable carrier as no rows. For\n    this filter both collapse toward minting, never toward suppression, but\n    the ruling below requires a present but unparseable snapshot copy to\n    refuse by name, so the filter reads the copy through a read that tells an\n    unparseable copy from an absent one.\n- **Unchanged readers, under their established contracts:**\n  `acceptance_record.staged_spine_findings` (the pre-commit warn, which keeps\n  reading the unfiltered walk); the merge rung's act reads\n  (`acceptance_record.lane_acted_set`, `reattest_scope_refusal`,\n  `held_reattest_refusal`, reached from `integrate.py`); and the snapshot's\n  other readers (`adjudicate_act.py`, `adjudicate_brief.py`,\n  `assumption_rules.py`, `check_trajectory.py`, `gen_open_items.py`,\n  `hats.py`, `pending.py`, `record_observation.py`, `spine_rules.py`,\n  `trace.py`, `trunk_step.py`, all under `project-trajectory/scripts/`). Their\n  contracts are IF-123 and IF-220 in `docs/requirements/interfaces.toml`;\n  this row redefines neither.\n- **Ruling** (coordinator-2026-10-20 D-020, for the owner to confirm or\n  overrule): an absent ledger is no acts, so nothing is filtered; a present\n  but unreadable or malformed ledger or snapshot copy refuses the mint by name\n  (fail closed); a row the filter cannot prove settled, a missing snapshot\n  copy included, is minted. The filter only ever removes a row it can prove\n  was judged in the range.\n- **What the suppression binds to:** the row's identity (its id in its\n  registry); act-ledger entries added in the requested range, that is,\n  entries whose `seq` the ledger at the range's `before` revision does not\n  hold, for the `before`/`after` the merge slot or the sweep was given; and\n  the row's whole approved and routed traced cell set at the range's `after`\n  tree, compared with the row's copy in the snapshot at that same tree.\n  Nothing outside that range or that tree can suppress a row.\n- **A refusal on each route:** at the merge slot's post-merge arm the merge\n  has already landed, so the refusal says the mint was refused (not that\n  nothing was merged) and mints no row; once the named carrier is fixed in a\n  reviewed commit, the recovery is `intake.py sweep --before --after` with\n  `--after` at that fixing commit (the same range re-reads the same broken\n  blob; only a blob that could not be read and is then fetched is swept over\n  the same range). At `intake.py sweep` the refusal ends the sweep with no row\n  minted, as its other refusals do.\n- **Ruling for the squashed-landing case** (OI-119 ruled 2026-11-02 (a):\n  stated here, not in the Done-when, so the plan's pass record stays bound;\n  Done-when item 2 states the outcome): OI-115 ruled 2026-11-01 (a): the\n  lane rebases onto WI-967's landing, and settlement reads the registries\n  each re-attesting act copied from the act ledger, so a row amended after\n  its act still mints on a squashed landing as on an unsquashed one (dispute\n  sitting 007-ADJUDICATE-1ae50f4's FIX).\n- **The scope of Done-when item 5 under a later copy** (dispute sitting\n  013-ADJUDICATE-93526df's F2 FIX: stated here, not in the Done-when, so the\n  plan's pass record stays bound): a row edited after its act and restored to\n  its pre-range text in one cell still mints whenever no later act in the\n  range copies the row's registry, and always for an approved cell, which a\n  later act cannot copy without re-attesting the row. When a later act\n  re-attesting another row copies that registry, a routed traced cell that\n  moved in the range before the row's act and is restored after it is\n  re-anchored by that copy, and the row settles: the later-writer arm proves\n  only that the row's routed traced cells are the same at the range's two\n  ends.\n\n## Critique dispositions\n\nScope critique round 1 (Codex Sol, 2026-10-21; CHANGES-REQUESTED,\nfindings=4):\n\n- F1 (MAJOR, S4 frontmatter): adopted — \"planmode = \\\"single\\\"\". The row has a\n  `## Trust` section, touches two script families (`intake.py`,\n  `acceptance_record.py`) and promises behaviour over an input space; it adds\n  a code path, so the deletion exception does not apply.\n- F2 (MAJOR, S2 `## Trust`): adopted — \"The guard follows\n  `project-trajectory/PROCESS.md` §3, \\\"When a guard is owed\\\"\"; the Producer\n  states each failed-write shape (\"*Structured error:* ...\", \"*Failed call or\n  timeout* ...\", \"*Partial file:* ...\"); \"Consumers newly given the\n  authority, by path\" lists the `intake.py` / `integrate.py` routes and the\n  `acceptance_record.py` intermediary; \"Unchanged readers, under their\n  established contracts\" links IF-123 and IF-220; \"What the suppression binds\n  to: the row's identity ...; act-ledger entries added in the requested range\n  ...; and the row's whole approved and routed traced cell set at the range's\n  `after` tree\"; and \"A refusal on each route\" gives each consumer's failed\n  outcome. D-020's ruling text is unchanged.\n- F3 (MINOR, S1 Done-when): adopted — \"## Deliverables and order\": the filter\n  with its regressions and spine rows lands first, the workaround's removal\n  \"lands after the filter and its regressions pass\", and \"They stay one row\n  because pieces 2 to 4 are each true only once piece 1 has landed\".\n- F4 (MINOR, S3 Done-when/Trust): adopted — \"Coverage bound: the promise\n  reaches the ledgers, snapshot copies and ranges that supported workflows and\n  the kit's own routes produce ... A hand-edited act ledger or snapshot copy,\n  hand-chosen name aliases ... and sub-second timing windows are outside it.\"\n\r\n# docs/requirements/open-items.toml — the owner decision registry.\n#\n# One table per decision the owner owes or has ruled. `-000` example rows are\n# inert, the copy-ready placeholder convention every registry here uses.\n#\n# Contracts: IF-073 — the interface seam this file declares (process.md §8; row\n# of record in docs/requirements/interfaces.toml).\n#\n# Contract IF-073: one `[open_item.OI-###]` table per owner decision, carrying\n#     `title`, `status`, `raised`, `one_line`, `decision`, `blast_radius`,\n#     `options` and `recommendation`. `status` is `pending` or `ruled`. WORK\n#     ITEMS POINT TO OPEN ITEMS (WI-790): a work item that waits on a ruling\n#     cites the item in its own `needs` and reads `blocked` until the item\n#     leaves `pending`; an open item carries no work-item pointer. Rows filed\n#     before WI-790 may carry `wi_refs`: it is history, preserved as written,\n#     and no reader derives a relationship from it; new rows carry none.\n#     Every pending row is filed together with a queued work item citing it —\n#     only a pending row a queued work item cites renders as a brief card, and\n#     a pending row no queued work item cites is an error. RULING an item\n#     updates the rows that cite it IN THE SAME COMMIT: each citing row's\n#     Done-when gains the decided criteria citing the item (a gate on a\n#     person's act gets a confirmation criterion, e.g. \"OI-98 ruled\n#     2026-10-NN: the re-sync was performed\"), its `specref` leaves this\n#     registry for a real spec, and its `title`, `buildtier`, `sr_refs` and\n#     `safety_class` move where the ruling changes them — or the row closes.\n#     A commit that rules an item and leaves a citing row's Done-when\n#     untouched is refused. A ruled row is history, and the Decisions log is\n#     its record. `-000` example rows are dropped, and an absent registry\n#     renders an empty queue rather than failing, so a repo carrying no\n#     decisions still has the surface.\n#\n[open_item.OI-000]\ntitle = \"Example row - the shape of an owner decision brief\"\nstatus = \"pending\"\nraised = \"2026-01-01\"\none_line = \"\"\"the one-line form status.md projects - what is being decided, and the recommendation in a clause\"\"\"\ndecision = \"\"\"What is being decided, who raised it and when, and any context a ruler needs that is not obvious from the one-line.\"\"\"\nblast_radius = \"\"\"What breaks or changes if this is ruled either way - the honest cost, not the optimistic one.\"\"\"\noptions = \"\"\"Option (a) ... · Option (b) ... · Option (c) ... - each with its FOR and AGAINST, separated by the middot the other registries use for lists-in-cells.\"\"\"\nrecommendation = \"The driver's recommendation and WHY, in the same voice a reviewer would argue it.\"\n\n[open_item.OI-7]\ntitle = \"WI-123: review-cadence dial\"\nstatus = \"ruled\"\nraised = \"2026-07-12\"\none_line = \"rule WI-123 (review cadence) - RULED 2026-07-27: keep per-slice review, WI-123 retired\"\ndecision = \"\"\"phase-close 2x adversarial review instead of per-slice (owner-raised 2026-07-12; spec archived to docs/archive/specs/WI-123.2026-07-27.md at the ruling). Ruled 2026-07-27 by the owner: -previous evidence shows a per slice review is advantageous and not to deviate.-\"\"\"\nblast_radius = \"\"\"the unattended loop's defect-catch latency - per-slice reviews are the escalation sensor the medium-BUILD relax leans on.\"\"\"\noptions = \"adopt phase-close cadence now · keep per-slice · wait for evidence.\"\nrecommendation = \"\"\"rule only after >= 2 phases of medium-BUILD evidence. The evidence arrived and points the other way: per-slice adversarial review caught real defects on WI-297, WI-313, WI-316 and again across 124/125/126-REVIEW-A - including a class self-review cannot catch (fluent, confident, false claims). Owner ruled KEEP PER-SLICE; the dial is retired rather than left standing.\"\"\"\nwi_refs = [\"WI-123\"]\nruled_date = \"2026-07-27\"\nruling_ref = \"docs/log.md#decisions-log\"\n\n[open_item.OI-10]\ntitle = \"Retire open-items.md for one generated HTML reference view\"\nstatus = \"ruled\"\nraised = \"2026-07-26\"\none_line = \"\"\"rule whether open-items.md is retired outright for a single generated HTML owner surface - ruled (b): o
