{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"agent_message","text":"Reconciled F2 against the settled code and amended only Drafted rows.\n\n- `LLR-364` — `detail`\n  - Before: “...the second that nothing routed moved for a later copy to absorb...”\n  - After: “...the second proves the row's routed traced cells are the same at the range's two ends... The second arm's one limit: a routed traced cell moved before the re-attesting act and restored after it is re-anchored by a later act's copy of the registry ... and the row settles.”\n  - Why: states the actual endpoint-equality guarantee and the restored-pointer limit, without claiming no intermediate routed movement.\n\n- `LLR-364` — `rationale`\n  - Before: “Suppressing a record without both a new re-attestation and equality to its approved copy would hide a later amendment; treating a present unreadable carrier as absence would make the suppression decision unauditable.”\n  - After: same text, plus: “The later-writer arm accepts one bound rather than minting on every later copy of the registry: a routed traced cell restored between two acts is re-anchored by the later copy, as the snapshot already re-anchors any traced change without a verdict.”\n  - Why: records the enduring tradeoff behind the permitted limit.\n\n- `TC-403` — `method`\n  - Before: “...leaves the row settled only when its routed traced cells are unchanged across the range; and the replayed landings mint nothing.”\n  - After: “...leaves the row settled only when its routed traced cells are the same at the range's two ends; the arm's one limit is pinned: a routed traced cell moved before the re-attesting act and restored after it settles when a later act re-attesting another row copies the same registry, over an unsquashed and a squashed range, while the same restore with no later copy of the registry still mints over both; and the replayed landings mint nothing.”\n  - Why: covers the new limit regression and its contrast in capability voice.\n\n- `TC-403` — `expected`\n  - Before: “Satisfies SR-178 AcceptanceCriteria through the LLR-364 filtering contract, both arms of its later-writer condition included over squashed and unsquashed ranges, and IF-091 call outcome.”\n  - After: “Satisfies SR-178 AcceptanceCriteria through the LLR-364 filtering contract, both arms of its later-writer condition and the second arm's stated limit included over squashed and unsquashed ranges, and IF-091 call outcome.”\n  - Why: aligns the expected outcome with the added limit coverage.\n\nBuilder-list reconciliation:\n\n- Confirmed `LLR-364.code_symbol` and `LLR-278.code_symbol` trace-cell corrections; left them untouched.\n- Confirmed the two new TC-403 evidence functions exist; all 21 evidence functions resolve.\n- Confirmed the code implements endpoint equality, including the restored-pointer settlement case and no-later-copy minting contrast.\n- Confirmed the claim copy’s Trust section scopes Done-when item 5 under a later registry copy; no registry change needed.\n\nChecks:\n\n- `check_trajectory.py --strict` — exit 0; `check_trajectory: clean`.\n- `trace.py --strict` — exit 0; `orphans=0 integrity=0`.\n- Pytest skipped as directed.\n\nNo decomposition round; no hat outcomes apply."}}
